WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.720
Speaker 1: Well, we have to start today with the&nbsp;
little red wave that couldn't. What was that kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.240
story? The little engine that could. This was&nbsp;
very much the opposite. This ended up being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:19.440
little red wave that was a ripple so small that&nbsp;
Democrats may actually gain seats in the Senate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.180
Democrats have won the Senate. Yes. We do not yet&nbsp;
know the results of the House. We're going to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.380
on the bonus show about the specific scenarios&nbsp;
that could end up prevailing in the House. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.800
over in the Senate, it is over in the sense that&nbsp;
Democrats have indeed secured a majority. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:44.820
question now becomes, is it a 5050 majority with&nbsp;
Kamala Harris as the tie breaking vote, or is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:52.440
a 5149 majority, which would be very important.&nbsp;
If we want to make Joe Manchin irrelevant and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:59.340
are going to get to that. But the final numbers in&nbsp;
the Nevada Senate race coming in over the weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:05.700
as well as the calling of the Arizona race. And&nbsp;
this is how Democrats got to 50. Mark Kelly did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:12.720
defeat Blake Masters in Arizona. There is still 6%&nbsp;
of the vote to be counted there. But with Kelly's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:21.060
lead of over 125,000 votes, it has been called.&nbsp;
This is really great news for the country when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:26.640
it comes to being marginally less humiliated on an&nbsp;
international stage. Blake Masters, the challenger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:34.080
to Mark Kelly, the man who won the Republican&nbsp;
primary, an absolute humiliation, just a pathetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:41.040
embarrassing, horrible can that it didn't make any&nbsp;
sense from the extreme MAGA wing. Almost as bad as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.000
Kerry Flake, quite frankly. And we'll get to Kerry&nbsp;
Lake. Don't worry. We have plenty on Kerry Lake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.280
coming up a little bit later. That was number 49&nbsp;
we have in Georgia, as we will talk about Raphael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:02:00.000
Warnock and Herschel Walker. December 6th, there&nbsp;
will be a runoff. Much of our focus and attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.060
and resources are going to go there. And number&nbsp;
50, indeed, from the state of Nevada, where Cortez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:14.580
Masto, the Democratic incumbent, has come out&nbsp;
ahead of challenger Adam Laxalt. It is a 6500 vote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:22.020
lead with 98% in, it has been called. There is not&nbsp;
a 50% rule in the state of Nevada. Remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:28.200
in Georgia there is a runoff because of Georgia&nbsp;
law that says the winner must get 50% of the vote.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:39.000
In Nevada, there is no such law. And with 48.8% of&nbsp;
the vote, Cortez Masto is number 50 and Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.720
have won the Senate. Now there is going to be all&nbsp;
sorts of, after the fact, armchair quarterbacking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:52.200
prognostication, re prognostication, whatever&nbsp;
the case may be. A couple of storylines that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.700
are coming up and some of them I've dealt with&nbsp;
myself on Twitter, which, by the way, is barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.860
functional anymore under Elon Musk Twit. Twitter&nbsp;
is, quite frankly, a disaster at this point. Let's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:09.420
put that aside. Some of you coming forward and&nbsp;
saying even to me, sir, you were wrong about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:16.920
coming red wave. You were. You need to apologize.&nbsp;
As usual, some in my audience obsessed with me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:23.040
even though I have nothing to do with this. Let's&nbsp;
review what had been my message in the last 3 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:30.000
4 weeks. Going into this election, I told you&nbsp;
the momentum has shifted in the direction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.760
Republicans. The momentum has shifted.&nbsp;
That being said, many individual races,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:42.360
when you look at their average polling, have a&nbsp;
bunch of right leaning polls included in them. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:50.940
it is making me and others suspect that some of&nbsp;
these forecasts, which include these right leaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:57.240
polls, may overstate the degree of momentum in the&nbsp;
direction of Republicans. And in the last week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:04.680
I very specifically said, whatever is the truth&nbsp;
about these polls, it's over. The polls are a tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:11.160
earlier on to give us a sense of the direction&nbsp;
in momentum when we are seven, five, three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.860
one day away from the election, if the polls are&nbsp;
right or the polls are wrong, we have to go and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:22.200
vote. And thanks to a lot of 18 to 20 or 29 year&nbsp;
olds, which we will talk about a little bit later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:29.400
the red wave was at most a red ripple. We&nbsp;
will see what happens in the house very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:35.100
very soon. But Democrats will control&nbsp;
the Senate. Now the question is 50 or 51.

